If we apply a composite index on three columns (created_date, role_type, another_col), and in the WHERE clause we use all three columns,
 but change their order — for example, sometimes role_type first, then created_date,
  then another_col — will the indexing work the same or differently?

Good question 👍 this is an important detail about composite (multi-column) indexes.

📌 How Composite Index Works

Let’s say you create a composite index on 3 columns:

CREATE INDEX idx_user_role_date_other
ON my_table (created_date, role_type, other_col);


The index is ordered left → right.
This means MySQL (or any RDBMS) can efficiently use it only if the leading column(s) are included in your WHERE clause.

✅ Cases where the index is used

Query with all three columns in any order:

WHERE created_date = '2025-09-12' AND role_type = 'ADMIN' AND other_col = 123


👉 Order in WHERE doesn’t matter — optimizer can reorder them, index will still be used.

SELECT *
FROM users
WHERE created_date = '2025-09-12'
  AND role_type = 'ADMIN'
  AND other_col = 123;

Even if you write:

WHERE role_type = 'ADMIN'
  AND other_col = 123
  AND created_date = '2025-09-12'

👉 The order in the WHERE clause does NOT matter.
The query optimizer (MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, etc.) rearranges conditions internally.



⚠️ Cases where performance differs

If your index is (created_date, role_type, other_col):

✅ This works (uses full index):
WHERE created_date = '2025-09-12' AND role_type = 'ADMIN'


⚠️ This does not use the index efficiently:
WHERE role_type = 'ADMIN' AND other_col = 123

Because created_date (the first column in the index) is skipped.


📊 Rule of Thumb
Composite index works left to right:

Index (A, B, C) can be used for:
(A), (A, B), (A, B, C)


But not directly for:
(B), (C), (B, C)

🔎 Your Case

If you query with all three columns, order in the SQL doesn’t matter — index will work the same.
But if sometimes you filter by role_type first without created_date, then the index (created_date, role_type, other_col) won’t help much.

In that case, you might consider:
Separate indexes on role_type (or role_type, created_date)

Or use a covering index that matches your most common query pattern.